---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import SamplingGate from '@/components/SamplingGate.astro'
---

<Doc
  title="Sampling"
  description="The five sampling strategies mcpd implements, why the decision is made once at the root, why the deterministic hash lets a fleet agree, and how head and tail decisions differ."
  lede="Keeping a fraction of the traces, without keeping a fraction of each one."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>The decision is made once, at the root</h2>
  <p>
    A trace is kept or dropped whole. The decision is taken when the root span
    starts and inherited by every child, so a run is never half recorded.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the rule everything else follows from. A trace with a random 30% of
    its spans missing does not read as a sampled trace. It reads as a bug in the
    system you are observing: a tool call that apparently never happened, a
    model call with no parent, a gap where the retrieval should be. You will
    spend an afternoon on it before you work out that the gap is your
    observability tool and not your agent.
  </p>

  <h2>The default keeps everything</h2>
  <p>
    With no sampling configured the policy is <code>always</code>, and that is
    the right default until volume says otherwise. Sampling trades away the run
    you will want to read. Pay that only when the storage or the bandwidth makes
    you.
  </p>

  <h2>The five strategies</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>always</code></td>
        <td>Keep everything. The default.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>never</code></td>
        <td>Keep nothing.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ratio</code></td>
        <td>Keep a fixed fraction, drawn at random once per trace at the root.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>deterministic</code></td>
        <td>Keep a fixed fraction, decided by hashing the trace id. Every collector reaches the same answer without talking to any other.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tail</code></td>
        <td>Defer the decision until the trace finishes, then keep every error and every slow run whatever the rate says.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Setting a rate</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--sample</code>, or <code>MCPD_SAMPLE</code>, takes a fraction from
    0.0 to 1.0 and configures the deterministic strategy. A value outside that
    range stops the daemon at startup with a message naming what you passed. It
    does not clamp, because a mistyped rate that quietly becomes 1.0 is
    discovered a month later by the storage bill.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    label="keep one trace in ten"
    command="mcpd --sample 0.1"
    primaryLabel="As a flag"
    secondary={[
      { label: 'as environment', command: 'MCPD_SAMPLE=0.1 mcpd' },
      { label: 'confirm what it read', command: 'mcpd config' },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Every SDK reads the same fraction, as <code>sample</code> in the client
    configuration or <code>MCPD_SAMPLE</code> in the environment. Sampling in
    the client is cheaper: a span that was not sampled is never queued and never
    exported, so it costs no bandwidth at all. Sampling in the daemon costs you
    the round trip but keeps the rate in one place, which is what you want when
    a dozen services report into one instance.
  </p>
  <p>
    The daemon samples with <code>always</code> or
    <code>deterministic</code>: <code>--sample</code> and a project's
    <code>sample</code> in the config file both set the deterministic strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Why hashing lets a fleet agree</h2>
  <p>
    Run several mcpd instances behind a load balancer and the spans of one trace
    do not all arrive at the same one. A sampler that flips a coin per instance,
    or per span, would keep some of a trace and drop the rest, which is the
    failure the first rule exists to prevent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deterministic strategy maps the trace id onto the full 64-bit range and
    keeps the trace when that value falls below the threshold the rate implies.
    Every instance computes the same number from the same id, so they all agree,
    with no shared state, no coordination protocol and nothing to fall out of
    sync. The same id also gives the same answer on a retry, so a replayed batch
    cannot half arrive.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two details keep that mapping accurate. It reads the <em>trailing</em> sixteen
    hex characters of the id: OpenTelemetry requires trace ids to be random, but
    some producers derive the leading bytes from a timestamp, and a sampler
    reading those would correlate with the time of day rather than sample. When
    the id is not hex at all, it falls back to a real hash with a finalising mix
    instead of degenerating into keeping everything, so the realised rate stays
    close to the rate you asked for.
  </p>

  <h2>An upstream decision wins in the SDKs</h2>
  <p>
    When an SDK continues a trace from an inbound W3C <code>traceparent</code>,
    it takes the sampled flag with the ids and does not apply its own rate. This
    holds both ways: the flag set keeps the trace whatever the local rate, and
    the flag clear drops it whatever the local rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    The daemon applies its own rate. Its OTLP door keeps the trace-flags byte on
    each span, in the attribute <code>mcpd.otlp.flags</code>, and a daemon with
    a rate applies that rate to every trace, whatever the flag says.
  </p>
  <p>
    The alternative is worse than it sounds. If each service applied its own
    rate, a trace crossing four of them would be kept by two and dropped by two,
    and what you would have at the end is not a distributed trace. It is a set
    of holes in the shape of one.
  </p>

  <h2>Head and tail decisions</h2>
  <p>
    A head sampler decides when the trace starts, from the trace id alone. It is
    cheap and holds nothing in memory, and the rate applies evenly: at a tenth,
    it keeps a tenth of the failures too.
  </p>

  {/* The pen stands 200 units in rather than at the component's default. An
      article column is 662px wide at every desktop size here, and the default
      arm stands at 760, so this chart had never once drawn its pen: the one
      element that makes a recorder a recorder was outside the field at every
      width, including a phone's. */}
  <SamplingGate class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    That is what a latency recording looks like: a flat baseline and a handful
    of excursions. The baseline is where the volume is and the excursions are
    where the answers are. A tail decision waits until the trace ends, then
    keeps it if anything in it errored or it ran longer than a threshold, and
    otherwise applies the same deterministic hash.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cost of a tail decision is buffering: it must hold every unfinished
    trace in memory until it can decide. The daemon's ingest path makes the head
    decision, so it holds no trace back.
  </p>

  <h2>Knowing what was dropped</h2>
  <p>
    Every span the sampler discards increments
    <code>mcpd_traces_sampled_out_total</code>, exported at
    <code>/metrics</code> in Prometheus text format. Write the rate down
    somewhere your team reads. A missing trace with a documented sample rate is
    an explanation; a missing trace with no explanation is a support ticket, and
    the person filing it is usually you.
  </p>

